-- 165 -- an entry on a CPU exception that pushes an error code (OFFEN O32 (9), Opus agent L).
--
-- A kernel's general-protection handler: `entry gp vector 13`. Vector 13 is one of the
-- exceptions (8, 10..14, 17, 21, 29, 30) for which the CPU pushes an ERROR CODE between the
-- saved state and the return address. Until Opus agent L the bare-metal runtime refused
-- such an entry (by the build and by `metall_idt_setze`): its common stub popped no code, so
-- `iretq` would have taken the code for the return address. The runtime now has the twin
-- stub (`metall_eintritt_gemeinsam_fc`, `laufzeit/metall/eintritt_asm.h`) that drops the
-- code before `iretq`; `gabbro build` and the freestanding stage bind such an entry with
-- `METALL_EINTRITT_FC` and install it with `metall_idt_setze_fc`, which takes ONLY those
-- vectors (and the plain installer keeps refusing them).
--
-- Stage 11 boots it (`metall165`): three non-canonical loads each raise #GP(0), the entry
-- runs, and the run continues -- the harness's C half steps the faulting instruction, the
-- one thing a Gabbro dispatch cannot do (it names no return address). The gift
-- (`metall165-gift`) builds the PLAIN stub onto the same vector: `iretq` takes the error
-- code for the return address, and the machine never reports.
--
-- What is not claimed: the error code does not reach the dispatch (an entry's `regs in`
-- name registers, and the code is on the stack -- the C half sees it in
-- `struct metall_rahmen_fc`); the declared `stack … ist 1` is not switched to (the entry
-- runs on the interrupted stack, OFFEN O32 (8)).

module beispiel::gp_eintritt {

entry gp vector 13 arch x86_64 {
    regs in  { }
    regs out { }
    preserves { rax, rbx, rcx, rdx, rsi, rdi, rbp, r8, r9, r10, r11, r12, r13, r14, r15 }
    clobbers  { }
    stack gp_stapel per cpu ist 1 nested never
    dispatch gp_verteiler;
}

impl fn gp_verteiler()
    effects { pure }
{
    return;
}

}
